riven by apis  and finally we want to talk about how we  get the things we need to run our  household  how do we get those necessities of daily  life where are we going to find them are  there enough in the store  can we order them online do we need to  have them delivered  do we need to go to the store and get  them these are all the kinds of  questions that people are asking  retail is one of those industries that's  been going through a digital  transformation for a long time now  and apis are an important part of what  they do as well  so let me talk about three companies who  are actually in these three particular  areas  and some innovative things that they're  doing with apis  the first company is change health care  change health care is not a health care  provider or a hospital conglomerate or a  doctor  they're a service provider to all those  people in particular they offer  something that they call the api and  services connection for healthcare  which is just what it sounds like it's a  set of apis  that these different kinds of healthcare  providers can use  in order to deliver things like health  eligibility calculations  patients responsibility calculations so  when you're going to go to the doctor  you can have some idea  how much you'll have to pay out of  pocket versus how much insurance will  cover  so basically they're a service provider  for all these health care companies that  want to offer a better digital patient  experience  and they're using apis for you know one  of the things that we've talked about  apis being useful for for many years  which is to allow third-party innovation  they provide a set of services  and their customers can then build on  those services to deliver innovative  technology for all of their consumers  now if we move on to food one company  that's no stranger to a digital  experience is pizza hut  pizza hut now has an app called the  pizza tracker and the pizza tracker  literally lets you keep track of your  pizza and believe it or not  on the super bowl which is the american  football championship which is a big  event in america  a lot of people want pizza and when they  order that pizza they want to know where  it is so pizza hut has to not only  deliver these new digital experiences  because people don't just want to call  the local pizza place and have someone  mumble back to them and  hope that their pizza is going to arrive  in time for the kickoff  they want to know that they're going to  get the exact pizza they ordered when  they ordered it  when they need it so for that this pizza  tracker is a new digital experience  they've created  and they've built it on top of a layer  of apis  that connects to systems that they  already had within the business for  tracking these things  in addition of course they have to deal  with these huge traffic spikes the super  bowl is the probably the biggest one  they've had to deal with to date  so they take advantage of all the things  underneath the api layer like auto  scaling  google kubernetes engine and other  technologies like that  so that when that demand spikes their  i.t systems don't struggle  and you don't give the customers a bad  experience instead you give them a good  experience  of giving them a responsive app that  lets them track that very important  thing  that's on their way to their house to  keep their friends happy and fit  and finally we'll talk about retail  retail's been doing digital things for a  long time  um but there are a lot of new things  that are happening conrad electronic is  one example they're actually in germany  and one of the things they did  is they took a lot of the data from  their existing legacy data warehouse and  moved it into bigquery  this is not a big query presentation  there are some great ones at  next i'm sure you can listen to but one  of the things they did with bigquery  then  was give access to those data warehouse  items  via an api by doing that now their b2b  customers can now use those apis on top  of bigquery  to have more timely and more accurate  information about conrad's offerings and  even their inventory  than they could have before because they  gave them a channel  for getting that information that's much  easier for developers to consume  in addition and we'll talk a little bit  about this pattern as well  once they had those apis they could  build a new in-store tool  and that in-store tool is something that  their associates in the store can use  in order to help customers better  understand what's in stock  what the price is and now when you come  to the store the people who work in the  store with that tool are better informed  than they ever were before resulting in  a better customer experience which i  imagine means  you know more people want to shop there  so that's a really cool example of using  an api for multiple contexts  both for b2b as well as to improve the  way things work internally  and improve their own customer  experience  so that's a little bit about how apis  are being used by all kinds of companies  and they all have apis at the core  really  and what we found is that you know i've  been talking about apis on and off since  about 2009  it began as something that people you  know were not sure  why we were talking about it like really  do we really want to offer these  external services  and now they've really become the way  that industries of all types communicate  and those examples are just some of the  examples  of the way that people are doing things  like building new mobile applications  building other kind of digital  applications and building  business-to-business services  based on apis but of course once you  build the api  you have to govern it you have to keep  track of it and you have to make that  api useful  and that's really where a lot of the  challenge of api management comes in  whenever we talk about apis at google we  almost always start with this little  chart you see here  which we call the digital value chain  and there are a couple of important  things about this one important thing  is of course it mentions the technology  after all an api  is a bit of technology that sits in  between a back end and an app  it's literally the connection between  the app and the back end  but an equally important part of this  value chain maybe even more important  is the connection between the people and  the connection that we're especially  interested in when we talk about apis  is that connection between the developer  who uses the api  and the api team or the people who  create the api  or manage or govern or even are  sometimes called the product managers of  the api  because making an api successful is more  than just sticking something on the  internet with an endpoint  and telling people to use it it includes  all kinds of  of aspects that a product manager has to  consider when they want to make their  api be useful  the interesting thing about this digital  value chain it also lets us focus  on sort of you know where the real value  is  so as technologists we always want to  start with the back end stuff  stuff we understand we have a legacy  system that  keeps track of our inventory for us  let's expose it as an api  okay what does the api look like well it  looks like our back-end system how do  you use it  i don't know do you know our back-end  system that's how you use it  that's not a really always the best way  to make some technology useful outside  the organization  but it's often where we start but it's  not always the most effective way what  about the developers  how are they going to use those apis  effectively what about the actual  customer  does the api do what they need so it  makes sense in another perspective often  to talk about an outside-in perspective  starting with really what do the  customers want um if it's a  business-to-consumer aspect  you have product managers you have  people in the organization  who probably have all sorts of great  ideas for new services they could offer  to their customers  going back to that pizza hut pizza  tracker app someone within pizza hut had  an idea to build that app  they had to come to a technology team  and say how are we going to build that  where's the information we need to build  that  well it may be locked inside legacy  systems in your company  and it may require some new apis to  expose it  as another example you might want to do  business to business like conrad did  you might want to actually make apis  that your large partners  can use to have a more efficient  relationship with you which  we hope would drive more business that  might drive a different kind of api  there might not only be a different set  of requirements for what the api does  but there could be different  requirements for the technology maybe  security has to work differently  maybe the data's got to be presented in  a different format  these are all questions that a really  effective api team  probably ought to be asking when they  start to do something with apis  so a great example of how to apply this  digital value chain  is to start with a real actionable  project and then move on  in order we did a webinar recently with  mtn  which is a multinational telco located  in africa and they've embraced apis and  i think they have a great example  for how you can actually use this  digital value chain in production  they started by solving the problem  right in front of them they needed some  specific point-to-point apps  and they created apis to support those  apps  but then they learned that they could  use those same apis for other things for  some of their web apps  to drive their call centers and then  after that they were able to have  additional  pieces of functionality that they could  build with their apis  like omnichannel retail like apis for  use by their partners  even eventually things like digital  assistance and iot  devices all of these things began to be  possible  as they started with a simple api and  started by solving a single business  problem  and then they branched out to build a  much more comprehensive platform  where today they have a wide variety of  apis and a wide variety of business  processes and this has really enabled  them to speed up  the ways in which their customers and  their own product managers can create  new things  and we've seen this example across a  whole lot of industries where  you know companies that used to you know  take months to  bring a new partner on board or create a  new marketing campaign  are able to do this sort of thing in  days or weeks because once you have the  api  and once you have the technology to make  it possible for people to consume it via  self-service  the cost to get a new consumer on that  api is extremely low  before you had apis and before you had  self-service it was a totally different  world  when you have to build a completely new  service or a new business or hire  software developers  in order to implement a new idea it's  much much much more expensive  than when someone can simply sign up for  an api via self-service using an  interface that a developer understands  well  and start working on it so what we find  is that the customers that succeed with  apis  end up sort of moving up this chain from  the left to the right  people often start out with apis to  solve point-to-point problems and that's  fine  we need to integrate system a and system  b let's do it with an api  so maybe once that integration is built  we can use it in other ways  or we need to build a a new mobile  application  let's build an api for that mobile  application but let's make it reusable  enough  that we'll be able to use it for other  applications in the future  and that's where we start to move up to  the right we move from basically giving  access to apis  to creating those differential digital  experiences like the pizza hut pizza  tracker  but where the real interesting stuff  comes is when people are able to use  those apis to create ecosystems  to create new types of business and to  enter in  new ways for people to interact with  them with them  as companies so there are a couple of  different reasons  why you might want to have an ecosystem  one is simply you may be able to unlock  new lines of business  um i mean going back i'm not sure  exactly what lines of business pizza hut  did but if i look for instance at  what we talked about with change  healthcare right they created a whole  new business  around offering api related services  for healthcare providers to use  those ecosystems also help you better  meet the needs of the customers and  that's a really important thing and they  help you discover new customers  those are really important because once  you've started to use an api to build an  ecosystem  you may find that some of the best  customer experiences  and some of the best new lines of  business for you could come  from people who are using your apis from  outside your company  we've talked for years about apis some  being something that you use to drive  third-party innovation and that remains  true  and we have a number of customers who  are doing that and doing that in some  very interesting ways  one great example of this area is  ticketmaster ticketmaster  is primarily u.s but i know they have an  international presence  and they're very successful at selling  tickets to online events  well real events now maybe at this  particular moment there aren't as many  of those selling as there once were  but that will come back and what  ticketmaster did is they've created an  api  and this has now enabled places like  retailers like costco  digital platforms like facebook you  could even imagine an airline or a hotel  company  saying hey when you book tickets at our  hotel you might also want to get tickets  to our local play using ticketmaster  and because they have those apis for  business-to-business interaction  they're actually able to create whole  new business channels  to enable them to be embedded in someone  else's digital experience  and a great example of that is that in  2018  15 of their ticket sales were coming  this way  so they've really seen that by creating  those apis and creating a platform or an  ecosystem  now third parties can do innovative  things with the services  that they already offer now a key to  making all this work  is what we call an api product which is  basically to say that  these apis are not just technology an  api is not simply a list  of urls and verbs and oauth tokens and  tls requirements and rate limits and  json that's the technology behind the  api  but when you build an api you're  creating it for somebody  you're creating it for a developer who's  going to build an app  so you have to think about all the  different aspects of that how are you  going to secure that  so that the right security mechanism is  used that for one your developers can  actually understand  but that meets the security requirements  for the kind of data that's in your api  you've got to think about how you're  going to monitor and analyze it so you  can tell  whether people are using that api  effectively whether it's bringing  business value  whether developers are being successful  you might even think about monetizing  your api which is you might find cases  where it makes sense to charge for an  api  that's not something that often comes up  but there are a number of companies who  have apis  that provide businesses where literally  on a transactional basis they're worth a  lot of money they provide  very detailed financial projections for  instance that's a great example for  monetizing an api directly  we find other cases like ticketmaster  where it may actually not make sense to  monetize your api because it's driving  more business to you  or you might even want to pursue a  profit sharing philosophy  where people who are using your api to  drive business to you might actually  take a share of your profits  we've seen people do all these kinds of  things with apis but the key to it  is to enable self-service for developers  that's really what apis and api  management are all about  it's not that difficult to put the  technology in place  to make it possible to call something in  your business over http  send it some json and get a result back  but now if you want  tens or hundreds or hundreds of  thousands of developers to use that api  either inside your company or outside  your company you've really got to think  about how they're going to learn about  it  how they're going to get access to it  and how they can do this via  self-service  if using your api requires going to a  meeting or attending a conference call  it's a lot less interesting than if it's  something you can do right there in your  web browser on your computer  okay now we talked about some examples  of using apis  we talked a little bit about apis as  products and the importance of  self-service  let's talk about how we do this and how  apis and api management  actually fit in an organization so  within an organization apis can be  everywhere  they can be in legacy systems they can  come from sas vendors they can be  third-party apis increasingly we see  more and more  people having third-party services  coming over the internet that are part  of the apis they're delivering even  inside a corporate network or internal  applications  of course we have vms we have legacy  systems we have serverless platforms we  have kubernetes  we have all kinds of technology but one  thing that's becoming increasingly clear  in all sizes of organizations all those  bits of technology  can be used to deliver apis and apis can  sit on all of them  and they can talk to each other and  clients can talk to them and all kinds  of things can happen  and the more and more apis an  organization has  the more important it is they can be  able to keep track of them to have some  sort of governance over them  so that a product manager or even a  security person can ask  questions like what apis do we have  how effectively are they being reused  are they secured in the appropriate way  um which ones are the most popular how  many apis do we have that nobody is  using  these are the kinds of things that  people bring an api management platform  infor  because by registering apis with the api  management platform  and getting statistics and analytics  from that platform  it's actually possible to answer some of  these questions and many organizations  find out they have apis they didn't know  about  or they have apis that they do know  about but aren't being used effectively  and this is a big important step for  customers and companies that are using  apis internally  to drive all sorts of innovation of  course once we start to drive the apis  outside the organization it gets even  more interesting because now we not only  have to deal with what happens inside  but what happens outside adds a few new  layers of complexity  for instance the developers using the  api are not necessarily part of your  organization so you need to figure out  how you're going to secure that api for  their use  do they work with an existing identity  platform or do you create a new one just  for them  if developers are going to use an api  via self-service they might be using  some sort of developer portal  a website where they can learn about  apis and sign up for access  how do you secure that how do you  maintain identity there  how do you make sure your api  documentation is effective and your api  designs are effectively communicated  so whereas internal api management and  governance tends to be about things like  enforcing api design standards  enforcing api security standards and  keeping track of what's going on  api management outside the organization  now includes things  like how do i market the api how do i  make the api easy for developers to  consume  um how do i add additional levels of  security so for instance  in a lot of cases people who have apis  on the internet that don't require  security credentials to use like some  retail apis that let you get prices in  store locations  many of those are subject to attacks by  bots and these are not bots that are  doing a denial of service or trying to  hack your system  these are people who have signed up for  your api like legitimate users  but they're using it to scrape data for  their own competitive purposes  so for instance in the travel industry  let's try to book a hotel room at all  the hotels and see what price the  competition is offering there are a lot  of different and interesting kind of  security threats and other things that a  company has to take into consideration  when they offer apis outside the  organization  and one of the roles of an api  management system is to help you manage  those sorts of things as well  so now we're not only helping you govern  and maintain standards inside the  organization  we're also helping you effectively make  sure those apis are used  are used well and are used in a secure  way by people who are outside your  organization  and once you share apis outside an  organization  that's when all of the fun things we  talked about before like governance  and the ability to create an ecosystem  and the ability to enable third-party  innovation  really comes into play and where you can  really do some interesting things as an  organization  now if we jump back inside for one more  second i don't always like to leave this  without talking a little bit about the  role of internal services  and especially microservices there have  been a lot of cases recently where  people have  started to use apis internally and then  some of the organizations that are  getting a little bit more advanced have  been talking for the last few years  about microservices about breaking  individual bits of functionality  into many many smaller bits that are  then deployed  separately that may run on different  technology platforms  that may be programmed in different  languages but which communicate  most often using api technology using  http  and json or perhaps using something like  graphql  or grpc all of these things counts as  apis  and all of this microservices stuff is a  really important step  in the evolution of businesses to become  more agile and to do things more quickly  but you don't want to end up in a  situation where as we show here  we actually have a lot of micro services  that talk to each other  but we have a number of ones that we  outlined in red which  are actually the microservices that talk  to other microservices  and what we often find is that when an  organization is doing microservices  development at scale  a particular team really delivers an api  or a few apis and that api is made  visible to other teams  that team might choose to implement that  api on top of a java app server  or they might choose to implement a  mainframe or they might choose it to  implement with  five microservices or ten microservices  or a hundred microservices but for that  team to expose all 100 of those  microservices  as they are directly to all the other  teams in the company is a recipe for  disaster and chaos  so what we often find a pattern that  works very well is to create api facades  for those groups of microservices  expose that api to the other parts of  the organization  and then what's underneath the api can  change it might be that monolithic java  app today  and it might be 100 applications using  grpc and golang tomorrow  but the interface for that service and  that team to the rest of the  organization doesn't change  that becomes a real important principle  that companies can use  as they start to explore some of the new  and more agile ways to use apis and  microservices technology  and finally i want to talk about google  and some of our offerings  we've been working hard for a long time  to offer a wide variety of products in  the api and api management space  on one side of the slide we see apogee  apogee is the api management product  we've offered for a few years now  i've been working on apogee since 2007  and it's been part of google cloud  since 2016. we have a new product as  well called api gateway  it's a fully managed api gateway and in  its current form  it's especially well suited for turning  serverless workloads like that forest of  microservices we just saw  into coherent apis and we find that in  many cases people will really want to  take advantage of this api gateway  because it's it's small it's fast it's  easy to consume and it's fully native  and fully managed  as part of google cloud apigee has many  of those attributes but apogee is also  capable of full life cycle api  management  managing everything in your api program  from the definition of apis  to publishing on developer portals to  monetization and advanced analytics  and over time you'll see us adding more  capabilities to both of these products  in order to give it a really good set of  capabilities so that our customers with  wide varieties of api needs  can find the right solution in google  cloud  so finally thank you thank you on behalf  of everyone at google cloud for joining  me today  and may you have some really awesome  digital journeys in the future

